A structured version keeps the mapping explicit instead of hoping the model weaves citations into prose reliably:

```js
async function synthesiseStage(question, findings) {
  const response = await client.messages.create({
    model: 'claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929',
    max_tokens: 800,
    messages: [{ role: 'user', content:
      `Question: ${question}\n\nSources:\n${findings.map((f, i) => `[${i}] (${f.url}): ${f.extracted}`).join('\n\n')}\n\nWrite each claim as a separate item with the source index it came from. Format as JSON: [{"claim": string, "sourceIndex": number}]`
    }]
  });
  const claims = JSON.parse(response.content[0].text);
  return claims.map(c => ({ ...c, sourceUrl: findings[c.sourceIndex]?.url }));
}
```

Every claim in the output carries its own `sourceUrl`, generated in the same call that produced the claim — there's no separate step where the two could drift apart, and nothing gets rendered to the user without an attached, checkable source.

