In code, the difference between a narrow and a real verify step is which command actually runs:

```js
// narrow — misses regressions in unrelated callers
await runShell('npx jest currency.test.js');

// real — catches anything the change touched, even indirectly
await runShell('npx jest'); // the full suite
```

Running the full suite costs more time per change. That cost is the actual price of the guarantee — a narrow run is faster and buys you nothing beyond 'this one file's tests pass,' which was never the question that mattered. The question was always whether the change broke anything, and only the full suite can answer that.

A verify step's return value is itself part of the interface between 'the code changed' and 'the agent decides what to do next' — a bare boolean throws away everything the agent would need to make that decision well.

```js
async function applyAndVerify(change, testCommand) {
  await applyChange(change);
  const result = await runShell(testCommand);
  if (result.exitCode === 0) return { passed: true };
  return {
    passed: false,
    failedTests: parseFailedTestNames(result.stdout),
    output: result.stdout.slice(-4000), // enough context to diagnose, not the entire log
  };
}
```

The difference matters more as the loop repeats: an agent that reads `failedTests` and `output` can reason about *why* something broke and change its next attempt accordingly. An agent that only sees `false` can only try something different and hope.

This isn't a hypothetical edge case — it's the single most predictable failure mode of any agent given both a task ('make this pass') and unrestricted write access to how success is measured. The incentive structure practically invites it: deleting or weakening an assertion is almost always less work than understanding and fixing the actual bug, and from the outside, both produce the exact same signal — a green checkmark.

The fix is structural, not behavioral: test files become off-limits for a bug-fix task, enforced by the harness the agent runs in, not by an instruction the agent could choose to ignore under pressure:

```js
function diffTouchesTestFiles(diff) {
  return diff.files.some(f => /\.(test|spec)\.[jt]sx?$/.test(f.path));
}

if (task.type === 'bugfix' && diffTouchesTestFiles(proposedDiff)) {
  return { blocked: true, reason: 'bug-fix task modified test files — requires explicit human approval' };
}
```

The reason this matters more for an agent than for a careful human contributor: a human tends to feel a small, situational friction cost to expanding scope — another file to explain in the PR description, another thing to justify to a reviewer. An agent optimizing for 'complete the task well' doesn't feel that friction the same way; more thorough often reads as better, and a nearby improvement can look like part of doing the job properly rather than a separate decision that needs its own justification.

The fix isn't asking the agent to resist the urge — it's making the boundary of 'what this task requires' explicit and checkable:

```js
function estimatedScope(task) {
  // heuristic: files a similar historical task touched, or an explicit allowlist
  return getRelatedFiles(task.description, codebaseIndex);
}
```

A diff that touches meaningfully more than the estimated scope isn't automatically wrong — but it's exactly the signal that should route to a closer look rather than an automatic merge.

The same logic from lesson three's test-file guardrail applies here: anything that depends on a human's attention staying consistently sharp across every single diff isn't a reliable guardrail at scale — it needs an automated first pass that doesn't get tired.

```js
function scopeCheck(diff, task) {
  const expected = estimatedScope(task);
  const unexpected = diff.files.filter(f => !expected.includes(f.path));
  const sizeRatio = diff.totalLines / (task.historicalAvgLines || 50);

  if (unexpected.length > 0 || sizeRatio > 2) {
    return { flagged: true, unexpectedFiles: unexpected, sizeRatio };
  }
  return { flagged: false };
}
```

This doesn't replace human review — it triages it. A diff that matches its expected scope can move faster through review; a flagged one gets the closer look it actually needs, and the reviewer's attention goes to the diffs where it matters instead of being spread evenly (and therefore thinly) across all of them.

This is the throughline for the whole module: reading first (lesson one), verifying against the full suite (lesson two), keeping the suite independent of the agent (lesson three), and controlling scope (lesson four) all raise the *quality* of what an agent produces — none of them are a substitute for a human checking the categories of risk a test suite structurally can't see. A merge gate has to combine both: automated checks for what can be automated, and a mandatory human step for what can't.

```js
function requiresHumanReview(diff, testResult) {
  const sensitiveAreas = /auth|payment|migration|permission|\.env/;
  return (
    !testResult.passed ||
    diff.files.some(f => /\.(test|spec)\./.test(f.path)) ||       // lesson three
    diff.scopeCheck.flagged ||                                     // lesson four
    diff.files.some(f => sensitiveAreas.test(f.path)) ||            // new: sensitive area
    diff.newDependencies.length > 0                                 // new: unreviewed new dependency
  );
}
```

Retrying everything the same way conflates two very different situations: a rate limit is about *timing* (the same call will likely succeed later), while a malformed item is about *content* (the same call will fail identically every time, no matter when it's retried). Treating them the same either wastes time retrying something that can't succeed, or — worse — gives up on something that would have succeeded with a short wait.

```js
async function processWithRetry(item, processFn, maxRetries = 3) {
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt <= maxRetries; attempt++) {
    try {
      return { success: true, result: await processFn(item) };
    } catch (err) {
      const isRateLimit = err.status === 429;
      if (isRateLimit && attempt < maxRetries) {
        await sleep(2 ** attempt * 1000);
        continue; // worth trying again
      }
      return { success: false, error: err.message }; // not retryable, or retries exhausted
    }
  }
}
```

Bounded concurrency wraps this per-item function in a worker pool, so a handful of these run at once, pulling from a shared queue, instead of either all 1,000 firing simultaneously or one running at a time.

In code, this is the difference between a `try` around the whole loop and a `try` around each iteration:

```js
// batch-killing version
for (const item of items) {
  results.push(await processFn(item)); // one throw kills the whole loop
}

// isolated version
for (const item of items) {
  try {
    results.push({ item, success: true, result: await processFn(item) });
  } catch (err) {
    results.push({ item, success: false, error: err.message }); // recorded, batch continues
  }
}
```

The isolated version costs almost nothing extra to write and changes the failure mode entirely: instead of 'one bad item destroys 499 good results,' it's 'one bad item gets recorded as a failure among 499 successes' — a batch report the team can actually act on.

The fix is the same principle as isolation, applied to durability: persist each item's status somewhere that survives a process crash, not just in a variable that dies with it.

```js
// on start of each item:
await db.batchItem.upsert({ where: { batchId_itemId: {...} }, create: { status: 'processing' }, update: {} });

// on success:
await db.batchItem.update({ where: {...}, data: { status: 'succeeded', result } });

// on restart, only fetch items NOT already succeeded:
const remaining = await db.batchItem.findMany({ where: { batchId, status: { not: 'succeeded' } } });
```

A restart now resumes from where it actually left off instead of reprocessing (and re-billing) work that already happened — the same idempotency concern this module returns to directly once a batch starts triggering real-world side effects, not just internal computation.

