This distinction matters more at volume than it would checking one record by hand, because at 2,000 records nobody is eyeballing each one — the validation layer *is* the check, and whatever it doesn't catch ships silently. A layered validator:

```js
function validateExtraction(record) {
  const errors = [];
  // structural: right shape
  if (typeof record.total !== 'number') errors.push('total: not a number');
  // semantic: right kind of value
  else if (record.total < 0) errors.push('total: negative — implausible for an order');
  if (!/^[^@\s]+@[^@\s]+\.[^@\s]+$/.test(record.email || '')) errors.push('email: not a valid email shape');
  return { valid: errors.length === 0, errors };
}
```

Structural checks (right type) and semantic checks (right range, right shape of value) run together, and a failure at either layer gets recorded with which one it was — 'not a number' and 'negative' point a reviewer at different problems even though both are `total` field issues.

The same principle that applied to a coding agent's verify step applies here: a failure signal is only as useful as the detail it carries. A validator that returns `false` forces every downstream consumer — a human reviewer, a retry step, a report — to redo the work of figuring out what actually went wrong, for every single failure, every time.

```js
function validateExtraction(record) {
  const errors = [];
  if (typeof record.name !== 'string' || !record.name.trim()) errors.push('name: missing or empty');
  if (!/^[^@\s]+@[^@\s]+\.[^@\s]+$/.test(record.email || '')) errors.push('email: not a valid email shape');
  if (typeof record.total !== 'number' || record.total < 0) errors.push('total: missing, wrong type, or negative');
  return { valid: errors.length === 0, errors }; // errors: [] on success, specific reasons on failure
}
```

At 2,000 records, this difference compounds: 60 bare `false`s is 60 records someone has to re-investigate from scratch; 60 records each with a specific, field-level reason is something a reviewer can triage in minutes.

This is the sharpest version of a distinction that runs through this whole module: some actions are safe to simply redo, and some aren't, and the difference is whether anything happens *outside* your own system.

- Recomputing a summary from stored data: safe to redo, no external trace.
- Re-reading a file to re-parse it: safe to redo.
- Sending an email, issuing a refund, calling a third-party API that changes state: **not** safe to redo without checking first — each redo is a new real-world event.

The protection is an idempotency check, done immediately before the side-effecting call, independent of whatever tracked the computation:

```js
async function sendConfirmationOnce(order) {
  const already = await db.emailLog.findUnique({ where: { orderId: order.id, type: 'confirmation' } });
  if (already) return { sent: false, reason: 'already sent' };
  await emailProvider.send(order.customerEmail, confirmationTemplate(order));
  await db.emailLog.create({ data: { orderId: order.id, type: 'confirmation', sentAt: new Date() } });
  return { sent: true };
}
```

Idempotency protects against *re-doing* a side effect that already happened. It says nothing about whether the very first run was correct. For a batch of actions with real consequences — refunds, bulk emails, deletions — a dry-run mode closes that gap: the agent computes and reports exactly what it would do for every item, with no side effect actually triggered, so a human can review the full scope before anything real happens.

```js
async function processRefunds(orders, { dryRun = true } = {}) {
  const plan = orders.map(o => ({ orderId: o.id, amount: computeRefund(o), reason: o.flagReason }));
  if (dryRun) return { dryRun: true, plan }; // report only, nothing issued
  for (const item of plan) {
    await issueRefundOnce(item); // the real, idempotency-protected action
  }
  return { dryRun: false, plan };
}
```

Dry-run first, human reviews the plan, then a separate explicit call with `dryRun: false` — never the same call deciding on its own that this run is the real one.

This closes the loop on the whole module: bounded, resilient processing (lesson one), per-item isolation and resumability (lesson two), layered validation (lesson three), and safe real-world actions (lesson four) all produce rich per-item detail along the way — a good report's job is simply not throwing that detail away at the last step by collapsing it into two numbers.

```js
function buildReviewReport(results) {
  return {
    summary: { total: results.length, succeeded: results.filter(r => r.status === 'succeeded').length, failed: results.filter(r => r.status === 'failed').length },
    failures: results.filter(r => r.status === 'failed').map(r => ({ item: r.item, reason: r.error })),
    needsReview: results.filter(r => r.status === 'succeeded' && r.confidence < 0.7).map(r => ({ item: r.item, confidence: r.confidence })),
    sideEffects: results.filter(r => r.sideEffect).map(r => ({ item: r.item, action: r.sideEffect })),
  };
}
```

This is the first and most important thing a user-facing agent needs: a hard boundary around what it will answer directly, set by the *consequences* of being wrong, not by how often it happens to be right. A support agent might be accurate on medication questions 95% of the time — that's still not a reason to let it answer them, because the 5% failure mode isn't a minor inconvenience, it's a real health risk, and nothing about the agent's confidence in the moment reliably signals which category a given answer falls into.

The boundary has to be explicit and enforced, not left to the model's judgment call in the moment:

```js
const offLimitsDomains = [
  /medication|drug interaction|dosage|diagnos/i,
  /legal advice|sue|lawsuit|liability/i,
  /investment advice|which (stock|fund)|retirement account/i,
];

function requiresHumanOrDisclaimer(message) {
  return offLimitsDomains.some(pattern => pattern.test(message));
}
```

This is deliberately a routing decision made *before* the model generates a substantive answer, not a check on the answer after the fact — the goal is never generating unearned authority in the first place, not catching and softening it after it's already been stated.

The trigger conditions themselves matter, but this lesson is about what happens once one fires: too many designs treat 'recognize the trigger' as the whole job and leave the actual disclosure vague — a stalling phrase instead of a clear statement. The fix is making the disclosure itself an explicit, required step, not an assumed side effect of recognizing the trigger:

```js
function escalationTriggered(conversation) {
  return isOutOfScope(conversation) || repeatedFailureCount(conversation) >= 2 || userExplicitlyRequestedHuman(conversation);
}

if (escalationTriggered(conversation)) {
  return {
    userMessage: "This needs a person to help you properly — I'm connecting you with someone now.",
    action: 'escalate',
  };
  // NOT: another reassuring 'let me check on that' that stalls without disclosing anything
}
```

A mood label plus a topic ('upset,' 'billing issue') tells a human almost nothing actionable — it's the same failure mode as a validation function that returns a bare `false`, or a batch report that only shows aggregate counts. What a receiving human actually needs is what was specifically tried, what specifically remains unresolved, and the concrete details (order numbers, dates, amounts) that let them pick up the thread without re-asking for information the user already gave once:

```js
function buildEscalationPackage(conversation) {
  return {
    userRequest: extractCoreRequest(conversation),
    attemptedSteps: conversation.turns
      .filter(t => t.role === 'assistant' && t.action)
      .map(t => ({ action: t.action, outcome: t.outcome })),
    unresolvedBecause: conversation.escalationReason,
    relevantContext: { accountId: conversation.accountId, orderIds: extractOrderReferences(conversation) },
  };
}
```

A warm handoff is specifically the version where the human doesn't have to ask the user to start over — which is only possible if the package carries real, specific detail forward, not a mood and a category.

