This is a design target, not a personality trait: a good disclosure of a limit has two required parts — the honest statement that this isn't reliably known, and something concrete to do about it. Dropping either part breaks it. Drop the honesty and you get confident fabrication. Drop the next step and you get an honest dead end that's barely more useful than fabrication, just less risky.

```js
function handleUnknownQuestion(question) {
  return {
    disclosure: "This isn't something I have reliable information on.",
    nextStep: canEscalate(question)
      ? 'Connecting you with someone who can help with this specifically.'
      : `You may find this in our documentation on ${likelyRelevantSection(question)}.`,
  };
}
```

This is the core risk of stopping at an aggregate trend: 71% overall could mean uniformly mediocre performance everywhere, or it could mean 95% on the easy, high-volume category propping up an average while the rare, highest-stakes category sits at 35% — two completely different situations that look identical in one number.

The fix is the same instinct as the data-agents module's validation lesson, applied to iteration instead of output-checking: break results down by category, not just in aggregate, before deciding the trend is good enough.

```js
function breakdownByCategory(evalResults) {
  const byCategory = groupBy(evalResults, r => r.correctUrgency);
  return Object.fromEntries(
    Object.entries(byCategory).map(([cat, results]) => [
      cat,
      results.filter(r => r.predicted === r.correctUrgency).length / results.length,
    ])
  );
}
```

Reactive controls protect against the second incident, not the first — which is precisely backwards from what a deployment plan should guarantee. The concrete version isn't a vague intention to monitor; it's specific, working code and thresholds that exist before the first real run, with numbers chosen deliberately (not just copied from a template):

```js
const DEPLOYMENT_CONTROLS = {
  dailySpendCapUSD: 50,          // enforced by the harness, halts new runs if crossed
  maxStepsPerRun: 25,             // hard ceiling, prevents infinite tool-call loops
  errorRateAlertThreshold: 0.15,  // checked hourly, not daily
  killSwitch: 'ENV.AGENT_ENABLED', // single toggle, checked at the start of every run
};
```

This is the concrete deliverable this module's lab asks for — not a description of what controls *should* exist in principle, but working thresholds and mechanisms in place before deployment, the same discipline as every guardrail this whole track has built toward.
